<section class="shell band">
  <h1>Privacy policy</h1>
  <div class="answer">
    <p>This page covers <strong>this website</strong>: what it collects, what it does not, and
    who else sees anything when you visit. Last updated {{ site.privacyUpdatedLabel }}.</p>
    <p>It does not cover your clinical record. How information from therapy or supervision is
    kept, used and disclosed is set out in the practice's <strong>Notice of Privacy
    Practices</strong> and the <a href="/contact/forms/">Limits of Confidentiality</a> notice,
    both given to you before your first session. Those are the documents that govern your care.
    This one governs a web page.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--night">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>In short</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Analytics</dt><dd>None. There is no Google Analytics, no tracking pixel and no third-party script on this site.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Cookies</dt><dd>None set by this site.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Accounts</dt><dd>There are none. Nothing here asks you to register.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Forms</dt><dd>The appointment form opens your own email app. Nothing is submitted to this website or stored by it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Payment</dt><dd>No part of this site accepts card or bank details.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Advertising</dt><dd>None, and no data is sold or shared with advertisers.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band">
  <div class="split">
    <div>
      <h2>What happens when you visit</h2>
      <p>This site is a set of static pages served by Cloudflare. Like every web server,
      Cloudflare records basic request information: the IP address the request came from, the
      page requested, the time, and the browser's user-agent string. That is used to serve the
      site and to protect it from abuse. It is not combined with anything else and it is not
      used to build a profile of you.</p>

      <h3>Fonts</h3>
      <p>Two typefaces load from Google Fonts. That means Google's servers receive your IP
      address and browser details when a page loads, as they would for any site using the
      service. No other third party receives anything.</p>

      <h3>What the pages themselves store</h3>
      <p>Nothing, with one exception. The <a href="/book/practice/">seven-step practice</a> can
      save what you write in it so the page remembers where you were. That is stored by your own
      browser, on your own device, and never leaves it. It is not sent anywhere, it is not
      readable by this practice, and the page has a button that deletes it.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>The appointment form</h2>
      <p>The form on the <a href="/contact/appointment/">request an appointment</a> page does not
      submit anything. It assembles what you typed into a message and opens it in your own email
      program, where you can read it, change it, or close it without sending. Nothing reaches
      this website, and nothing is stored here either way.</p>
      <p>That is deliberate. A web form on a therapy practice's site is a tempting place to
      write something private, and the safest version is one that cannot keep what you write.</p>

      <h3>Email is not secure</h3>
      <p>Ordinary email travels through servers neither of us controls. Use it to arrange a
      consult, not to describe what you are dealing with. Anything clinical belongs in the
      session.</p>

      <h3>Children</h3>
      <p>This practice sees adults aged 18 and over, and this site is not directed at children.
      No information is knowingly collected from anyone under 18.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="band band--sand">
  <div class="shell">
    <h2>Your clinical record</h2>
    <p>Records of therapy and supervision are held in the practice's clinical record system, not
    on this website. They are protected by HIPAA and by Texas law, and the practice's Notice of
    Privacy Practices sets out how they may be used and disclosed, and the narrow circumstances
    in which disclosure is required without your consent.</p>
    <p>You can ask for a copy of your record in writing, and it is provided electronically within
    15 business days of the request. To have records sent to another clinician, a doctor or a
    school, use the <a href="/contact/forms/">authorization for release of information</a>.</p>
    <p>A complaint about a Texas licensee goes to the Texas Behavioral Health Executive Council
    on 1-800-821-3205.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="shell band faq">
  <h2>Questions about privacy on this site</h2>

  <details>
    <summary>Does this website use cookies or track me?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. There is no analytics package, no advertising pixel and no cookie set by this site.
      The only third party involved in loading a page is Google Fonts, which receives your IP
      address and browser details in order to serve two typefaces.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>If I fill in the appointment form, where does it go?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Into your own email program, as a draft you can edit or discard. It is not submitted to
      this website, not stored here, and not passed to any form service. If you close the draft
      without sending it, nobody sees it, including this practice.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Is what I type into the self-care practice page private?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>Yes. It is saved by your browser on your own device so the page can remember where you
      were, and it never leaves that device. This practice cannot see it. The page has a control
      that deletes everything saved.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>Does this policy cover my therapy records?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>No. This page is about the website. Your clinical record is governed by the practice's
      Notice of Privacy Practices, which you receive before your first session, together with the
      Limits of Confidentiality notice setting out when disclosure is legally required.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details>
    <summary>How will I know if this policy changes?</summary>
    <div class="answer-body">
      <p>The date at the top of this page changes. Material changes to how the site handles
      information will be described here rather than made quietly.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

